<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Qi</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 577 CE · capital Ye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Gao family's dynasty in north-eastern China, rich and cultured but famously unstable, whose law code and Buddhist art shaped the Sui and Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> had dominated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a> from behind a puppet emperor since 534. In 550 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a> deposed the last Eastern Wei ruler and proclaimed himself emperor of Qi at Ye, inheriting the wealthy and populous plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, with a second power centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The court mixed a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> military elite with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, and rivalry between the two groups ran through its politics. Several emperors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a> above all, were notorious for cruelty and drunkenness, and palace purges were frequent. Yet the state was prosperous: its Code of 564 became a model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished, as the cave temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangtangshan-caves">Xiangtangshan</a> and the sculpture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qingzhou">Qingzhou</a> show.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the last effective ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-wei">Gao Wei</a>, misrule and the execution of able generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulu-guang">Hulü Guang</a> weakened the army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> invaded in 576 and took Ye in 577, reuniting northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a step towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> reunification of 589.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-qi.jpg" alt="Northern Qi" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Northern_Qi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 577 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gao Yang (Emperor Wenxuan), Gao Zhan (Emperor Wucheng), Gao Wei</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qi (Gao clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=564&amp;focus=northern-qi" aria-label="Northern Qi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7559 869 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Qi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7879 1125L7855 1179M7879 1125L7769 1191M7879 1125L6820 913"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7769 1191h0M6820 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7879 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7879 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ye. <a href="/atlas?year=564&amp;focus=northern-qi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
